  "preview": "A 24-song playlist on shuffle (with replacement). Predict how often a pop song plays in 600 spins, then chase compound events: 'pop AND rap back-to-back' and 'no pop in three songs in a row.'",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Move students from single-event theoretical probability and expected counts into compound events with independent trials. Build the multiplication intuition by contrasting it against students' default 'just add the probabilities' instinct.",
    "studentPrompt": "Maya's playlist has 24 songs. The breakdown:\n\n- 9 pop songs\n- 6 rap songs\n- 5 country songs\n- 3 lo-fi songs\n- 1 mystery track that auto-plays at the end of every album she owns\n\nShe plays the playlist on shuffle, which picks a song at random and then puts it back in the pool (every song has the same chance of playing next, every time).\n\n1. What is the probability that the next song is pop? Rap? Country? Lo-fi? Express each as a fraction in lowest terms AND as a percent.\n\n2. Maya streams 600 songs over the next month. About how many of each genre should she expect?\n\n3. Maya says, \"There's no way I'd hit the mystery track even once in a month.\" Is she right? Defend your answer with a probability calculation.\n\n4. What is the probability that the next TWO songs are BOTH pop? What about a pop song FOLLOWED BY a rap song? Show how you got each.\n\n5. What is the probability that NONE of the next three songs is pop? (Hint: what has to happen each time?)",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A stylized phone screen showing a music app with a circular shuffle icon and a stack of song tiles tagged by genre.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"15\" width=\"140\" height=\"190\" rx=\"16\" fill=\"#1f2937\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"28\" y=\"30\" width=\"124\" height=\"150\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#0f172a\"/><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"75\" r=\"26\" fill=\"#3f42d9\" fill-opacity=\"0.85\"/><path d=\"M78,75 l16,-10 0,20 z\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"36\" y=\"112\" width=\"108\" height=\"12\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#334155\"/><rect x=\"36\" y=\"112\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#22d3ee\"/><rect x=\"36\" y=\"132\" width=\"108\" height=\"8\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#334155\"/><rect x=\"36\" y=\"148\" width=\"108\" height=\"8\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#334155\"/><g transform=\"translate(60,168)\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"><path d=\"M0,4 l8,-4 0,8 z\"/><path d=\"M14,4 l8,-4 0,8 z\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"4\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"4\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#ef4444\"/><circle cx=\"64\" cy=\"4\" r=\"4\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(178,30)\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#0f172a\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"120\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fde68a\"/><text x=\"8\" y=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">Pop  9</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"28\" width=\"120\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fca5a5\"/><text x=\"8\" y=\"43\" font-weight=\"600\">Rap  6</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"56\" width=\"120\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#86efac\"/><text x=\"8\" y=\"71\" font-weight=\"600\">Country  5</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"84\" width=\"120\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#a5b4fc\"/><text x=\"8\" y=\"99\" font-weight=\"600\">Lo-fi  3</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"112\" width=\"120\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#1f2937\"/><text x=\"8\" y=\"127\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#fde68a\">Mystery  1</text></g><g transform=\"translate(178,178)\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#475569\"><text x=\"0\" y=\"0\">24 songs total</text></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the playlist breakdown. Ask: 'If I hit shuffle right now, what's the chance the next song is pop?' Once that lands, point at Q4: 'What about TWO pop songs in a row?' Don't explain anything. Send them to the boards.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: 24 colored chips or a deck of cards to model the playlist",
      "Optional: dice / spinner for simulation"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "probability",
      "compound-events",
      "independent-events",
      "expected-value",
      "complement"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Low floor (Q1 is pure ratios), high ceiling (Q5 needs complement reasoning OR three-factor multiplication). The diamond is the moment students realize compound events MULTIPLY instead of ADD. Q3 is a great launch for expected-value-as-prediction discussion.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: P(pop) = 9/24 = 3/8 = 37.5%. P(rap) = 6/24 = 1/4 = 25%. P(country) = 5/24 ~ 20.8%. P(lo-fi) = 3/24 = 1/8 = 12.5%. Strong groups also note P(mystery) = 1/24 ~ 4.17%.\n- Q2: Expected counts in 600 plays: pop 225, rap 150, country 125, lo-fi 75, mystery 25.\n- Q3: Expected mystery plays = 600 x 1/24 = 25. Maya is wrong by a wide margin. Strong groups note 'expected' is not 'guaranteed' but 25 is far from 0.\n- Q4: Some groups will ADD (9/24 + 9/24 = 18/24 = 75%). Wrong but predictable. Correct: P(pop then pop) = 9/24 x 9/24 = 81/576 = 9/64 ~ 14.06%. P(pop then rap) = 9/24 x 6/24 = 54/576 = 3/32 ~ 9.38%.\n- Q5: Two paths.\n  - Direct: P(not pop) = 15/24 = 5/8 each time, so (5/8)^3 = 125/512 ~ 24.4%.\n  - By complement: 1 - P(at least one pop), but that's harder. Most groups go direct.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Adding probabilities for AND events: P(pop AND pop) = 9/24 + 9/24. This is the most common error and is worth a dedicated mini-discussion.\n- Treating the shuffle as 'without replacement' (drawing songs from a shrinking pool) even though the prompt explicitly says songs go back in. Worth pointing out the prompt language.\n- For Q3, students arguing Maya is right because 'it COULD not happen.' Push them on the difference between possible and expected.\n- For Q5, computing 1 - P(pop) = 1 - 9/24 = 15/24 and stopping there, forgetting it has to be 'not pop' THREE TIMES.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Group A said P(pop then pop) = 18/24. Group B said 9/64. They cannot both be right. What's the difference between adding and multiplying probabilities, and when do we do which?\n- For Q3, Maya 'expects' 25 mystery plays. Could she actually hear 0? Could she hear 50? What does 'expected' really mean?\n- For Q5, would the answer change if shuffle did NOT put songs back? How would you set up the calculation then?\n- What if Maya added 6 more pop songs? Predict (don't compute) how each answer changes. Which answer is most affected?",
    "extensions": "- Maya wants the mystery track to play, on average, about once a week (so about 4 times in 30 songs). How many copies of the mystery track does she need to add to the 24-song playlist to hit that target? Show your work.\n- Maya's friend Theo claims his shuffle is 'broken' because he heard 3 pop songs in a row last night. Compute the probability of EXACTLY 3 pop songs in a row on Maya's playlist (then a non-pop). Does Theo have evidence the shuffle is broken?\n- SIMULATION: Use a spinner divided into 24 equal sectors (or roll a die, mapping outcomes to categories) to simulate 30 'shuffles.' Tally the results. How close do you get to the expected counts? Pool every group's data on the board. What happens as the total number of trials grows?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. P(pop) = 9/24 = 3/8 = 37.5%. P(rap) = 6/24 = 1/4 = 25%. P(country) = 5/24 ~ 20.8%. P(lo-fi) = 3/24 = 1/8 = 12.5%.\n\n2. Expected counts in 600 plays:\n  - Pop: 600 x 3/8 = 225\n  - Rap: 600 x 1/4 = 150\n  - Country: 600 x 5/24 = 125\n  - Lo-fi: 600 x 1/8 = 75\n  - Mystery: 600 x 1/24 = 25 (so all five categories total to 600 ✓)\n\n3. Expected mystery plays = 25. Maya is wrong. The probability of hearing the mystery track AT LEAST ONCE in 600 tries is essentially 1 (P(never) = (23/24)^600, which is microscopic).\n\n4. P(pop then pop) = 9/24 x 9/24 = 81/576 = 9/64 ~ 14.06%. We multiply because the trials are independent (replacement).\n   P(pop then rap) = 9/24 x 6/24 = 54/576 = 3/32 ~ 9.38%.\n\n5. P(not pop) on one play = 15/24 = 5/8. Three independent plays: (5/8)^3 = 125/512 ~ 24.4%. So there's roughly a 1-in-4 chance of going three songs without a pop song.",
